1
00:00:03,375 --> 00:00:04,436
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what was your question, eh?

2
00:00:04,896 --> 00:00:05,516
[SPEAKER_02]: Can you repeat it?

3
00:00:05,817 --> 00:00:06,477
[SPEAKER_02]: Meditation.

4
00:00:07,077 --> 00:00:14,542
[SPEAKER_00]: So, my question was that, see, I've been practicing meditation and I hear about meditations.

5
00:00:15,182 --> 00:00:20,666
[SPEAKER_00]: I've done two different techniques, but when you say meditation, I don't really know.

6
00:00:20,686 --> 00:00:22,547
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, even when I do it, I don't know.

7
00:00:22,587 --> 00:00:29,031
[SPEAKER_00]: I just do it, but if it's the proper way I'm doing it, if I'm just deluding myself, it's just...

8
00:00:30,851 --> 00:00:38,394
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, one thing to get out of the way is, only a sotāpanna can meditate properly.

9
00:00:40,395 --> 00:00:43,076
[SPEAKER_02]: So, there is no sama-samādhi without sama-dhīti.

10
00:00:44,897 --> 00:00:56,482
[SPEAKER_02]: So, coming from that point of view, you can see how silly the idea of a technique is, that you can teach to others, whereby they just emulate and mechanically repeat it.

11
00:00:57,392 --> 00:01:00,235
[SPEAKER_02]: kind of leads automatically to wisdom or something.

12
00:01:01,056 --> 00:01:01,477
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't.

13
00:01:01,517 --> 00:01:04,380
[SPEAKER_02]: It leads to an experience of novelty of a kind.

14
00:01:05,882 --> 00:01:12,550
[SPEAKER_02]: And, again, in connection to what I said a half an hour ago, previous recording was,

15
00:01:14,543 --> 00:01:29,006
[SPEAKER_02]: Repeating something thousands of times rightly, based on your discernment, based on your faith, you know that this, attending things like this, is most likely right, so you repeat, repeat, repeat, repeat it thousands of times, and then you get a confirmation, you get wisdom.

16
00:01:29,286 --> 00:01:32,507
[SPEAKER_02]: You discern, oh, it is right, what I've been doing.

17
00:01:33,947 --> 00:01:40,809
[SPEAKER_02]: So, getting a template technique, that can somehow lead you to that right state,

18
00:01:42,458 --> 00:01:46,101
[SPEAKER_02]: It's rather... well, it's obvious that it's kind of impossible.

19
00:01:47,422 --> 00:01:55,489
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if I tell you a technique that works for everyone and everything, it doesn't mean that... it doesn't require you to be discerning anything.

20
00:01:56,230 --> 00:02:03,976
[SPEAKER_02]: It just requires you to repeat these observances and these procedures that I've described for you.

21
00:02:04,056 --> 00:02:07,239
[SPEAKER_02]: Observe the nostrils when this happens, you do that, when you do that.

22
00:02:07,759 --> 00:02:08,560
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's not about

23
00:02:10,076 --> 00:02:14,402
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, understanding is not broad about doing certain things.

24
00:02:14,802 --> 00:02:22,132
[SPEAKER_02]: I'll tell you, you will understand, you will gain knowledge, if you're mindful of your shoulders, and you watch your breath in, and you watch your breath out.

25
00:02:22,692 --> 00:02:24,555
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, how would it bring any understanding?

26
00:02:25,742 --> 00:02:28,763
[SPEAKER_02]: At most, that will make you experience certain things.

27
00:02:29,003 --> 00:02:33,504
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, a novelty experience of your body in this shape, or that form, or whatever else.

28
00:02:34,124 --> 00:02:36,824
[SPEAKER_02]: But that has nothing to do with discerning and understanding.

29
00:02:37,324 --> 00:02:42,846
[SPEAKER_02]: Because in order to discern and understand, you need to know what you're supposed to discern and understand.

30
00:02:44,526 --> 00:02:54,608
[SPEAKER_02]: If I tell you, observe your act of breathing, seeing it as impermanent, seeing it as cause of suffering, well, that's a different thing.

31
00:02:55,534 --> 00:02:57,215
[SPEAKER_02]: But that's not really a technique, isn't it?

32
00:02:58,497 --> 00:03:06,864
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't say to somebody, oh, I'll try to understand things being given beforehand, which is the reason for you not being able to own them in the first place.

33
00:03:07,064 --> 00:03:08,565
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't present that as a technique.

34
00:03:09,726 --> 00:03:15,551
[SPEAKER_02]: I can say it, it can make sense to you, you can attend things like that, but that's the most that it can be.

35
00:03:15,691 --> 00:03:17,453
[SPEAKER_02]: And it doesn't need to be anything more than that.

36
00:03:18,614 --> 00:03:20,796
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you know, I know for a fact lots of people get upset.

37
00:03:21,640 --> 00:03:25,522
[SPEAKER_02]: When you dismiss the techniques or whatever, you say, oh, the techniques have been helping me or whatever.

38
00:03:25,582 --> 00:03:29,325
[SPEAKER_02]: You, as a pathogena, have no criteria of what help is.

39
00:03:29,345 --> 00:03:31,586
[SPEAKER_02]: Your criteria is, it feels good, feels better.

40
00:03:33,767 --> 00:03:36,089
[SPEAKER_02]: So, absolutely, the first step is to get the right view.

41
00:03:37,370 --> 00:03:38,470
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, you'll know how to meditate.

42
00:03:40,211 --> 00:03:44,014
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you don't have the right view, then your meditation should be about getting the right view.

43
00:03:48,517 --> 00:03:49,617
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, you know, when you say this,

44
00:03:51,848 --> 00:03:58,095
[SPEAKER_01]: Meditation is dwelling on the view that you have attained.

45
00:03:59,796 --> 00:04:04,261
[SPEAKER_01]: Dwelling on my view, dwelling on your right view, is meditation.

46
00:04:04,381 --> 00:04:06,143
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah, exactly.

47
00:04:06,183 --> 00:04:08,525
[SPEAKER_01]: So, but there is other meditation.

48
00:04:09,746 --> 00:04:11,648
[SPEAKER_02]: There are many types of meditation, yeah.

49
00:04:12,538 --> 00:04:13,839
[SPEAKER_02]: But only this one is the right one.

50
00:04:14,199 --> 00:04:23,363
[SPEAKER_02]: As the Buddha said himself, I think I spoke there with Arundalina, when he said, you premeditate, overmeditate, undermeditate, mismeditate, and so on, but he doesn't meditate.

51
00:04:24,205 --> 00:04:31,189
[SPEAKER_02]: And then he compares it to a dog waiting for a hare, or a jackal, or a cat waiting for a mouse, preying upon something.

52
00:04:31,209 --> 00:04:33,011
[SPEAKER_02]: That's usually what people's meditation is.

53
00:04:33,171 --> 00:04:37,133
[SPEAKER_02]: And you realize, if you look at all these techniques that have been taught, that is actually in common.

54
00:04:37,694 --> 00:04:44,938
[SPEAKER_02]: It's all about by observing, observing, looking as close as possible, praying, waiting, waiting, and then it happens.

55
00:04:46,099 --> 00:04:51,102
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's all about getting an experience of a novelty kind, anything.

56
00:04:52,453 --> 00:04:54,334
[SPEAKER_02]: And no doubt you will, sooner or later.

57
00:04:54,394 --> 00:04:54,634
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

58
00:04:54,694 --> 00:05:03,118
[SPEAKER_02]: Because all the all the efforts you're putting in, all the efforts in close observation, all you're going to get is something you get when you look at things closer.

59
00:05:04,039 --> 00:05:10,802
[SPEAKER_02]: But if that's all that's required for wisdom, and nibbāna and understanding, you wouldn't need the Buddha to tell you how to do it.

60
00:05:10,842 --> 00:05:18,626
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, you know, the whole formulation of the dhamma in like a great great detail and so on, none of that would be necessary then for anyone.

61
00:05:20,032 --> 00:05:22,894
[SPEAKER_02]: Because all you have to do is just look at things closer.

62
00:05:23,394 --> 00:05:29,697
[SPEAKER_02]: If you look at things closer, as a patrujana, with your ignorance, all you're doing is looking closer at your ignorance.

63
00:05:34,860 --> 00:05:38,342
[SPEAKER_02]: And no knowledge can arise from that.

64
00:05:39,262 --> 00:05:46,466
[SPEAKER_02]: Because people sometimes think, oh, you just need to drop everything, just sit and be like a baby still, or whatever else.

65
00:05:47,327 --> 00:05:49,808
[SPEAKER_02]: Don't judge, just go back to the whatever.

66
00:05:50,818 --> 00:05:54,740
[SPEAKER_02]: Primordial state, and that will teach you what you need to know.

67
00:05:54,980 --> 00:05:55,721
[SPEAKER_02]: That's ridiculous.

68
00:05:57,642 --> 00:05:58,162
[SPEAKER_02]: Through and through.

69
00:05:58,663 --> 00:06:01,124
[SPEAKER_02]: Experience, we talked about being affected by avijjā.

70
00:06:01,744 --> 00:06:07,227
[SPEAKER_02]: Means whatever you choose to stay with in your experience, will be affected by that avijjā.

71
00:06:07,368 --> 00:06:09,209
[SPEAKER_02]: Everything is corrupted for putuṣṇa.

72
00:06:11,130 --> 00:06:14,151
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why I said, you can't accidentally step outside of your ignorance.

73
00:06:14,292 --> 00:06:14,532
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

74
00:06:14,612 --> 00:06:16,693
[SPEAKER_02]: Because a repetition is required.

75
00:06:18,890 --> 00:06:26,877
[SPEAKER_02]: And in order to know that the repetition of something will be... is the right thing to get you out of ignorance, you need to be told.

76
00:06:26,897 --> 00:06:27,898
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, you need to do it.

77
00:06:33,564 --> 00:06:38,308
[SPEAKER_02]: And doing is not just sitting and staring and waiting for things to teach you.

78
00:06:39,089 --> 00:06:41,731
[SPEAKER_02]: Because things are affected with ignorance as much as you are.

79
00:06:42,011 --> 00:06:42,232
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

80
00:06:42,272 --> 00:06:43,693
[SPEAKER_02]: Because they're part of your experience.

81
00:06:46,117 --> 00:06:48,438
[SPEAKER_02]: So, everything, top to bottom, is affected with the vijjā.

82
00:06:49,038 --> 00:06:53,440
[SPEAKER_02]: So, even when you sit and meditate like that, all you're going to do will be meditate around your vijjā.

83
00:06:55,781 --> 00:07:02,444
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is not accidental that those people, who are very much in technique, would get upset if somebody dismisses their techniques.

84
00:07:04,665 --> 00:07:05,506
[SPEAKER_02]: Why would you get upset?

85
00:07:06,926 --> 00:07:11,208
[SPEAKER_02]: Satipaṭṭhāna can't get upset if somebody disparages the dhamma or whatever.

86
00:07:11,228 --> 00:07:14,810
[SPEAKER_02]: He will say, oh, well, he will clearly know that person doesn't know what they're talking about.

87
00:07:15,530 --> 00:07:20,452
[SPEAKER_02]: But it wouldn't upset him.

88
00:07:20,472 --> 00:07:22,093
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just impossible to take it personally.

89
00:07:23,894 --> 00:07:40,262
[SPEAKER_01]: When you say that also that the the only... if you're not a self-punisher, doing all sorts of techniques, well, okay, it doesn't matter, but the best thing you can do, which is a kind of meditation in a way, is like...

90
00:07:41,127 --> 00:07:49,371
[SPEAKER_01]: They are trying to understand, trying to dwell on the suttas and go through them, to understand each point so thoroughly.

91
00:07:49,451 --> 00:07:51,332
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a meditation in the proper sense of word.

92
00:07:52,013 --> 00:07:53,554
[SPEAKER_00]: Reflecting with your own experience.

93
00:07:53,714 --> 00:07:54,214
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah.

94
00:07:54,234 --> 00:07:58,036
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, again, like even the first jhāna, which is, well, that's the meditation.

95
00:07:58,256 --> 00:08:00,237
[SPEAKER_02]: It's thinking and pondering.

96
00:08:00,297 --> 00:08:04,840
[SPEAKER_02]: Or as the Annāmalai Thera translated it, thinking and exploration of the right things.

97
00:08:06,601 --> 00:08:08,121
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's just, it's just basically being...

98
00:08:10,859 --> 00:08:20,084
[SPEAKER_02]: Whether you meditate or not, it's... whether... when I say meditate, whether you sit down, cross-legged or whatever, whichever posture you prefer, you're doing the same thing.

99
00:08:21,105 --> 00:08:25,508
[SPEAKER_02]: You're pursuing the right view and then the principles of the right view, of the right insight.

100
00:08:27,489 --> 00:08:31,251
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can do it more actively or more passively, but it's still the same thing.

101
00:08:32,697 --> 00:08:35,938
[SPEAKER_02]: So, when you get the right view, then you can sit and practice samatha.

102
00:08:36,258 --> 00:08:45,620
[SPEAKER_02]: But it doesn't mean forgetting about your right view and observing your kneecaps and shoulders on top of your head and roof of your mouth and nostrils and your lungs.

103
00:08:45,640 --> 00:08:46,320
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just ridiculous.

104
00:08:46,360 --> 00:08:57,603
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you can observe those things correctly from the saḷāyātana point of view and then see how your body is just a composure of equal external elements as the objects around you are.

105
00:08:58,430 --> 00:09:03,252
[SPEAKER_02]: upon which you directly stand as your mind, your personality, and everything else, and then disown it.

106
00:09:03,292 --> 00:09:03,492
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

107
00:09:03,552 --> 00:09:07,773
[SPEAKER_02]: Because eyes, roof of your mouth, shoulder, kneecaps, is all given beforehand.

108
00:09:08,594 --> 00:09:09,414
[SPEAKER_02]: What I was saying before.

109
00:09:10,474 --> 00:09:19,477
[SPEAKER_02]: Yet, your life, as you know it, your past, your future, is inconceivable to be inseparable, to be independent of this living body.

110
00:09:20,478 --> 00:09:24,599
[SPEAKER_02]: So, this living body, less than two meters tall,

111
00:09:25,511 --> 00:09:31,072
[SPEAKER_02]: Full of organs, like a, like a, like a, you know, sack of, sack of beans tied at the top with just different types inside.

112
00:09:31,633 --> 00:09:33,553
[SPEAKER_02]: That is the reason.

113
00:09:34,113 --> 00:09:37,994
[SPEAKER_02]: That is the necessary cause, necessary condition for your whole life.

114
00:09:38,434 --> 00:09:41,175
[SPEAKER_02]: All emotions, everything you've been through, everything you will go through.

115
00:09:42,075 --> 00:09:45,616
[SPEAKER_02]: And you can't conceive it if there's not a living body.

116
00:09:45,636 --> 00:09:48,437
[SPEAKER_02]: Thus, if that body comes to die, everything comes to an end, in that sense.

117
00:09:49,517 --> 00:09:52,058
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you recognize that you have no input over that body.

118
00:09:53,875 --> 00:09:55,717
[SPEAKER_02]: No say, no control over it.

119
00:09:57,338 --> 00:09:59,900
[SPEAKER_02]: Any control you can exhibit is like what I spoke before.

120
00:10:00,481 --> 00:10:02,503
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a movement along the given lines.

121
00:10:03,744 --> 00:10:05,245
[SPEAKER_02]: Before, they were given beforehand.

122
00:10:07,947 --> 00:10:14,753
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then you realize your whole life, whatever you've done, whatever you've chosen to do, your volition, it's all determined by this external thing.

123
00:10:15,394 --> 00:10:18,196
[SPEAKER_02]: It's because of it, over which you have no say.

124
00:10:18,957 --> 00:10:21,299
[SPEAKER_02]: And now, you can't ask for better meditation than that.

125
00:10:22,691 --> 00:10:24,992
[SPEAKER_02]: Get that insight, get that thought, and then dwell on it.

126
00:10:25,293 --> 00:10:26,273
[SPEAKER_02]: Dwell on it, dwell on it.

127
00:10:26,353 --> 00:10:27,814
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the repetition I was talking about.

128
00:10:27,854 --> 00:10:28,695
[SPEAKER_02]: That's meditation.

129
00:10:29,655 --> 00:10:31,096
[SPEAKER_02]: Where meditation leads to wisdom.

130
00:10:31,457 --> 00:10:34,458
[SPEAKER_02]: Not where meditation leads to some mystical novelty states.

131
00:10:35,099 --> 00:10:38,601
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, I felt this trembling, or that trembling, I felt so peaceful, I felt this, I felt that.

132
00:10:39,041 --> 00:10:39,241
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

133
00:10:39,522 --> 00:10:43,524
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you get understanding at the end of your meditation?

134
00:10:43,864 --> 00:10:47,727
[SPEAKER_01]: Lots of people don't have that kind of ahma, I might give myself that.

135
00:10:48,729 --> 00:11:06,266
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're sitting and to go and go watch a thought or think about a subject, what seems to be better for the person is writing it out and actually writing things, not for publication, but actually writing that out, describing it as I see it.

136
00:11:07,767 --> 00:11:11,651
[SPEAKER_01]: In a way, that's what I think I should be doing with, obviously, with my... because as I'm doing that writing,

137
00:11:12,664 --> 00:11:15,226
[SPEAKER_01]: I have to bring those, that... You have to dwell on it.

138
00:11:15,266 --> 00:11:19,828
[SPEAKER_02]: You have to dwell on it until, yeah, until it crystallizes to the extent that you're able to put it into words.

139
00:11:20,109 --> 00:11:26,533
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't just let it, like, skim over, just... because you don't stop to kind of clarify anything for yourself.

140
00:11:28,174 --> 00:11:33,557
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, just clarifying, but it's difficult, because your mind is skipping this way, that way...

141
00:11:33,597 --> 00:11:36,419
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly, because you're not used to clarifying the nature of things.

142
00:11:36,859 --> 00:11:39,741
[SPEAKER_02]: So your meditation should enable you to do that.

143
00:11:41,557 --> 00:11:44,999
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's not accidental that meditation results in stillness of the mind.

144
00:11:45,019 --> 00:11:48,000
[SPEAKER_02]: It means clarity increases, the mind is jumping less.

145
00:11:48,040 --> 00:11:50,141
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you look at things more clearly.

146
00:11:51,562 --> 00:11:54,983
[SPEAKER_02]: Again, it's the same things you're trying to understand.

147
00:11:55,003 --> 00:12:02,967
[SPEAKER_02]: People have this notion whereby, okay, you know, you're trying to study, you contemplate, you reflect, and now it's a time for formal meditation.

148
00:12:03,568 --> 00:12:05,869
[SPEAKER_02]: When you don't do that, you do formal meditation.

149
00:12:06,029 --> 00:12:07,249
[SPEAKER_02]: What is formal meditation?

150
00:12:07,329 --> 00:12:08,610
[SPEAKER_02]: Sitting down with your robot.

151
00:12:12,200 --> 00:12:13,260
[SPEAKER_02]: What is formal meditation?

152
00:12:13,460 --> 00:12:14,281
[SPEAKER_02]: Following a technique.

153
00:12:15,261 --> 00:12:17,562
[SPEAKER_02]: What is formal like watching your breath or whatever?

154
00:12:18,122 --> 00:12:20,863
[SPEAKER_02]: But why do you do all of that in the first place?

155
00:12:20,903 --> 00:12:22,264
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you want to be free from suffering.

156
00:12:23,024 --> 00:12:27,266
[SPEAKER_02]: And who knows the way that leads you out of suffering?

157
00:12:27,326 --> 00:12:28,587
[SPEAKER_02]: It's at least a sotāpanna.

158
00:12:28,707 --> 00:12:33,389
[SPEAKER_02]: So, why don't you spend all of your time you have trying to become a sotāpanna?

159
00:12:35,870 --> 00:12:38,551
[SPEAKER_02]: Then you will know how to meditate and what to do and how to do it.

160
00:12:39,628 --> 00:12:49,532
[SPEAKER_01]: The thing is, but I notice as well, like I struggle to do breath meditation, but, you know, I try to do it, but then I don't.

161
00:12:51,873 --> 00:12:57,276
[SPEAKER_01]: My mind wanders off, whatever, but if I write about it, I end up being calm.

162
00:12:59,436 --> 00:13:03,838
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, because then I bring it in front of me, and that's the way I'm holding it all the time.

163
00:13:03,858 --> 00:13:04,479
[SPEAKER_01]: So it actually...

164
00:13:09,567 --> 00:13:11,968
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm doing it without doing it the traditional way.

165
00:13:13,269 --> 00:13:13,649
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

166
00:13:13,689 --> 00:13:15,170
[SPEAKER_01]: By writing about it.

167
00:13:17,010 --> 00:13:19,251
[SPEAKER_02]: Traditional way is the ignorant way.

168
00:13:19,712 --> 00:13:26,715
[SPEAKER_02]: Because the tradition is written by people, by fukujiners, by people who are not on the way to become arahants.

169
00:13:28,456 --> 00:13:30,076
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's not just an empty dismissal.

170
00:13:30,096 --> 00:13:33,978
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just that if you look at it and

171
00:13:34,990 --> 00:13:38,793
[SPEAKER_02]: People who compile commentaries and everything, they themselves said they were proficient.

172
00:13:39,634 --> 00:13:40,875
[SPEAKER_02]: Famous commentaries and so on.

173
00:13:42,876 --> 00:13:50,762
[SPEAKER_02]: You read assertives, even the techniques were such a crucial factor.

174
00:13:50,802 --> 00:13:52,083
[SPEAKER_02]: Why they wouldn't ever talk about it?

175
00:13:52,784 --> 00:13:54,165
[SPEAKER_02]: Why there is not a single technique?

176
00:13:55,946 --> 00:14:02,912
[SPEAKER_00]: It's something I've got to notice as well, like, the recent... a...

177
00:14:03,731 --> 00:14:22,277
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what I can understand, or what I can intellectually comprehend, is that, recently, the arahants, later on, didn't write any commentaries, or didn't do anything.

178
00:14:23,678 --> 00:14:26,879
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the kundalinas that kept on writing things and adding things.

179
00:14:26,959 --> 00:14:30,780
[SPEAKER_00]: It's that they noticed that there's no need

180
00:14:33,070 --> 00:14:36,671
[SPEAKER_02]: More than enough.

181
00:14:37,252 --> 00:14:46,615
[SPEAKER_02]: And if anything, all of the stuff that's been written later on, the religion is now obscuring those things, because it's been written so long ago, that now people think it to be the same.

182
00:14:48,756 --> 00:14:54,579
[SPEAKER_02]: But, you read the suttas and all the Buddha talks about all the time is understanding, developing understanding.

183
00:14:54,619 --> 00:14:56,480
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, parī, full understanding.

184
00:14:56,540 --> 00:14:58,400
[SPEAKER_02]: It always results in full understanding.

185
00:14:58,440 --> 00:15:00,721
[SPEAKER_02]: Whatever approach of the practice you take, full understanding.

186
00:15:01,182 --> 00:15:05,043
[SPEAKER_02]: What full understanding you get from having a mystical experience, it overwhelms you.

187
00:15:06,004 --> 00:15:07,545
[SPEAKER_02]: There's absolutely no understanding there.

188
00:15:07,605 --> 00:15:08,845
[SPEAKER_02]: Not even mindfulness often.

189
00:15:10,506 --> 00:15:17,049
[SPEAKER_02]: So, discernment, knowing the cause of suffering, like, knowing the gratification, knowing the escape,

190
00:15:18,499 --> 00:15:35,011
[SPEAKER_02]: In regard to sensuality, in regard to various mental states, knowing the origin, knowing the gratification, knowing the escape, like knowing, like really fully understanding, not analyzing, not getting all the facts, but fully understanding, penetrating with your mind, knowing it.

191
00:15:38,473 --> 00:15:40,535
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's all... that's what it's all about.

192
00:15:42,376 --> 00:15:43,837
[SPEAKER_02]: Understanding the nature of things.

193
00:15:46,922 --> 00:15:49,663
[SPEAKER_02]: Understanding the nature of things results in freedom from suffering.

194
00:15:52,224 --> 00:16:12,410
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'm going back to my earlier question that, if I say, with the current state of mind, if I'm being reflective about my thoughts and my actions, and if I'm trying to be as mindful as possible, is that meditation?

195
00:16:19,123 --> 00:16:20,243
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, you can say that.

196
00:16:20,403 --> 00:16:26,885
[SPEAKER_02]: But, obviously, be mindful as possible.

197
00:16:26,925 --> 00:16:34,086
[SPEAKER_02]: Again, the better you know what mindfulness is, the more established in mindfulness you are.

198
00:16:36,507 --> 00:16:45,029
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not just a state you emulate, you also want to understand what is presence of mind, what is the nature of it.

199
00:16:47,438 --> 00:16:50,020
[SPEAKER_02]: What else appears there when I think unmindful?

200
00:16:53,842 --> 00:16:54,723
[SPEAKER_00]: Manly ignorance.

201
00:16:55,703 --> 00:16:58,985
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, manly ignorance or ignorance of what?

202
00:16:59,145 --> 00:17:00,907
[SPEAKER_02]: What you see directly, you don't.

203
00:17:03,148 --> 00:17:08,411
[SPEAKER_02]: But you want to... you always... you want to be... you want to be like investigating at the same time, not just kind of...

204
00:17:10,897 --> 00:17:14,260
[SPEAKER_02]: When you sit and meditate, again, you want to meditate to get the right view.

205
00:17:14,621 --> 00:17:21,227
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't get the right view accidentally, so you can't just sit and be mindful of here and now, present moment, and then learn from it.

206
00:17:22,168 --> 00:17:29,014
[SPEAKER_02]: The only way you can learn from it is if you know what you're supposed to learn, if you know what you're supposed to look for in things.

207
00:17:29,355 --> 00:17:31,517
[SPEAKER_02]: Otherwise, it's just... you're just staying with your ignorance.

208
00:17:32,398 --> 00:17:35,921
[SPEAKER_02]: And you'll be as ignorant on the thousandth day as you were on a day one.

209
00:17:39,146 --> 00:17:51,097
[SPEAKER_02]: So, reading the suttas, and then if you want to sit and meditate, like, read the suttas, and then there will be a path that will kind of make sense to an extent, or at least seem promising that you might go.

210
00:17:51,998 --> 00:17:52,979
[SPEAKER_02]: There is something to this.

211
00:17:53,039 --> 00:17:54,881
[SPEAKER_02]: I could... What is it meant?

212
00:17:54,921 --> 00:17:56,582
[SPEAKER_02]: I could perhaps understand this better.

213
00:17:57,603 --> 00:17:59,545
[SPEAKER_02]: And then take that to your meditation.

214
00:18:01,206 --> 00:18:04,269
[SPEAKER_02]: And then dwell on it, in that sense that Tanya was saying.

215
00:18:05,336 --> 00:18:07,538
[SPEAKER_02]: Don't just let it, you know, slip or slide.

216
00:18:07,618 --> 00:18:10,700
[SPEAKER_02]: If you need to, you write it down or whatever, but you want to clarify it.

217
00:18:12,021 --> 00:18:28,975
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the thing, like, you know, I remember when I was doing work meditations on Agarika a lot, and you kind of just, you know, spondering on the suttas and various things, and I might have said this many times before, and, you know, like the five aggregates, and then you just think about the five aggregates.

218
00:18:29,836 --> 00:18:31,057
[SPEAKER_02]: But then it occurred to me, well,

219
00:18:32,268 --> 00:18:45,902
[SPEAKER_02]: When I read the suttas that say consciousness or they talk about matter or feelings, why do I think that I understood by default what consciousness refers to?

220
00:18:46,562 --> 00:18:47,944
[SPEAKER_02]: What feelings refer to?

221
00:18:49,285 --> 00:18:54,911
[SPEAKER_02]: Whatever other aggregate that Buddha mentions, why do I... why did I assume that I know what that refers to?

222
00:18:56,771 --> 00:18:59,753
[SPEAKER_02]: And then I suddenly think, okay, so what is the matter then?

223
00:18:59,893 --> 00:19:06,118
[SPEAKER_02]: Can I explain to myself what rūpa is, in my experience, right here, right now, while I'm explaining it?

224
00:19:07,839 --> 00:19:10,882
[SPEAKER_02]: And then I went like that for every, every aggregate.

225
00:19:11,705 --> 00:19:14,166
[SPEAKER_02]: And you realize that you don't really have a clear idea.

226
00:19:15,066 --> 00:19:15,967
[SPEAKER_02]: You just assumed.

227
00:19:16,647 --> 00:19:22,529
[SPEAKER_02]: You just picked up and you have a vague kind of ambiguous, concocted concept of it.

228
00:19:23,069 --> 00:19:30,252
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you stop and think, okay, when I say consciousness-consciousness, or if I say feeling-feeling, what does that exactly stand for?

229
00:19:31,279 --> 00:19:32,260
[SPEAKER_02]: Is there a clarity there?

230
00:19:32,320 --> 00:19:32,821
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't know.

231
00:19:33,422 --> 00:19:35,444
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, you realize, well, that's what the practice is.

232
00:19:36,085 --> 00:19:45,516
[SPEAKER_02]: Undoing all these wrong views, and wrong concepts, and misconceptions, and misperceiving the things that you just took for granted now.

233
00:19:45,856 --> 00:19:48,659
[SPEAKER_02]: So, now, when even you come... even when you come to practice,

234
00:19:49,360 --> 00:19:57,647
[SPEAKER_02]: The the stuff you read in the suttas, well, you approach it with same polluted concepts, tools, not understood things, that you never questioned.

235
00:19:57,667 --> 00:20:00,429
[SPEAKER_02]: You just start building on top of them, your practice of dhamma.

236
00:20:00,769 --> 00:20:17,643
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's no wonder you end up following weird, various, intense techniques, searching after, preying upon, like those animals in the suttas, that the Buddha says, they prey upon its prey, wait for it, for novelty states, for mystical experiences to take you over,

237
00:20:18,850 --> 00:20:21,713
[SPEAKER_02]: As a form of validation or confirmation of what you've been doing.

238
00:20:22,214 --> 00:20:28,720
[SPEAKER_02]: So, your whole spiritual life would be misdirected, because you never actually re-evaluated the very kind of basics.

239
00:20:30,021 --> 00:20:36,708
[SPEAKER_02]: And when you did, if you did, if you were, then you would realize, oh, the practice is actually in a completely different direction.

240
00:20:36,868 --> 00:20:41,173
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the direction of undoing, so that things can be recognized correctly.

241
00:20:42,327 --> 00:20:52,976
[SPEAKER_02]: Not in finding out and rediscovering of something, like something has been obscured and then you just... No, it's... well, it was obscured, you can say it was obscured, by your own misconceiving.

242
00:20:54,537 --> 00:21:06,246
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you want to be able that when you say to yourself, feeling, feeling, that statement, that designation stands directly for that which feeling is in your experience right there and then.

243
00:21:06,286 --> 00:21:09,509
[SPEAKER_02]: If there is not that clarity, there you go.

244
00:21:09,809 --> 00:21:11,050
[SPEAKER_02]: There's a work laid out for you.

245
00:21:12,790 --> 00:21:17,532
[SPEAKER_02]: And you can specifically take feelings, because craving is regard to feelings, and that's the whole point.

246
00:21:18,312 --> 00:21:23,915
[SPEAKER_02]: Learning how to feel and overcoming the feeling, thus never ever again experiencing suffering.

247
00:21:25,195 --> 00:21:30,317
[SPEAKER_02]: The way I said, my teaching is for the one who feels, and he teaches only two things.

248
00:21:30,837 --> 00:21:32,018
[SPEAKER_02]: Suffering and freedom for me.

249
00:21:38,441 --> 00:21:41,662
[SPEAKER_00]: It's actually where I find a little bit of an advantage

250
00:21:42,467 --> 00:21:44,308
[SPEAKER_00]: You get closer, closer to the meaning.

251
00:21:44,328 --> 00:21:51,750
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same sense, when you kind of, you know, you look at the Pali, you learn the Pali, then you look at different translations, different angles.

252
00:22:03,948 --> 00:22:08,449
[SPEAKER_02]: That all becomes necessary, but not for a scholastic purpose, but for the purpose of clarifying.

253
00:22:08,469 --> 00:22:15,091
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you see, it's not about, again, finding out some divine providence, a mystical light.

254
00:22:16,311 --> 00:22:21,672
[SPEAKER_02]: It's about undoing your wrong views, so that you recognize what the right view is.

255
00:22:23,313 --> 00:22:25,653
[SPEAKER_02]: And when it comes to undoing, that's the whole point.

256
00:22:26,454 --> 00:22:31,515
[SPEAKER_02]: You need to have as clear as possible instructions, so you'll know what to look for.

257
00:22:33,088 --> 00:22:34,269
[SPEAKER_02]: Like the utterance of another.

258
00:22:34,309 --> 00:22:36,490
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, the Buddha's not around anymore.

259
00:22:36,730 --> 00:22:41,432
[SPEAKER_02]: And, actually, even when he was, he referred to a miracle of instruction.

260
00:22:42,233 --> 00:22:48,016
[SPEAKER_02]: So, being able to convey the dhamma to somebody, and they fully understand it, and then become arahant or khandhā, equals to a miracle.

261
00:22:48,716 --> 00:22:50,237
[SPEAKER_02]: Why, Beth, when you see yourself?

262
00:22:50,277 --> 00:22:58,962
[SPEAKER_02]: Because we all take things and terminology to mean, you know, sometimes completely, sometimes slightly different things.

263
00:23:01,296 --> 00:23:08,902
[SPEAKER_02]: Even a slight variation in what it means for me, when I utter it for him, can be quite a lot at the end of the line.

264
00:23:09,382 --> 00:23:19,469
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, imagine a boat or a ship is setting on a course, and in the beginning, you know, it doesn't matter if it's a meter to the left, a meter to the right.

265
00:23:20,270 --> 00:23:27,275
[SPEAKER_02]: But after, you know, thousands of miles of journey, that tiny little kind of variation will result in missing a whole continent.

266
00:23:29,440 --> 00:23:32,521
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why you want to get as much, because you don't have a criteria what's right or wrong.

267
00:23:32,561 --> 00:23:38,463
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you want to try to clarify for yourself as much as you can.

268
00:23:41,945 --> 00:23:50,808
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then you kind of decrease the chances of being wrong later on, or at least not go too far off.

269
00:24:07,254 --> 00:24:12,575
[SPEAKER_01]: I like the, what's it, the concept of mindfulness, the thinking of mindfulness, what that is.

270
00:24:13,795 --> 00:24:17,896
[SPEAKER_01]: It's thinking about it, clarifying that, which is quite helpful.

271
00:24:19,137 --> 00:24:21,217
[SPEAKER_01]: Because then you can look at things better.

272
00:24:21,937 --> 00:24:25,538
[SPEAKER_01]: You can try to do certain things better once you know how to be mindful.

273
00:24:26,178 --> 00:24:26,398
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

274
00:24:26,978 --> 00:24:32,380
[SPEAKER_02]: But... Because most people, most people, most people think, oh, I can be mindful, I just mean being here and now, present.

275
00:24:32,920 --> 00:24:33,960
[SPEAKER_01]: But then you kind of

276
00:24:35,024 --> 00:24:43,427
[SPEAKER_02]: Vinaya, you... Well, exactly, because when you... you realize, yeah, you can be mindful, but if you have lots of wrong views, you'll be mindful with your wrong views.

277
00:24:44,568 --> 00:24:46,969
[SPEAKER_02]: And it's actually quite true.

278
00:24:47,009 --> 00:24:54,732
[SPEAKER_02]: When you take an average person sort of being mindful, if you were to ask them to describe it, well... well, first of all, they probably wouldn't be able to.

279
00:24:55,352 --> 00:25:02,395
[SPEAKER_02]: But second, it will all revolve around the wrong views of... as if I'm in the present moment, as if the present moment is sort of...

280
00:25:03,974 --> 00:25:22,751
[SPEAKER_02]: In a constant change, constant flux that I step into, just through reminding myself of mindfulness, then also another belief comes that, well, if I just have enough, a sufficient amount of my mindful mind moments, then I'll be, I'll reach nibbāna automatically or whatever else.

281
00:25:23,412 --> 00:25:27,396
[SPEAKER_02]: But you realize, well, mindfulness is obviously the absolute necessity.

282
00:25:28,717 --> 00:25:28,857
[SPEAKER_02]: But

283
00:25:29,610 --> 00:25:40,420
[SPEAKER_02]: If you're not knowing where to, how to undo your avijjā, all you're going to be mindful is, or rather, whatever you're mindful of, will be mindful of with your avijjā.

284
00:25:40,820 --> 00:25:42,181
[SPEAKER_02]: You'll be ignorant in regard to it.

285
00:25:44,623 --> 00:25:52,871
[SPEAKER_02]: So, mindfulness needs to... well, mindfulness and authenticity, like self-transparency, being honest to yourself, is the first step.

286
00:25:54,340 --> 00:25:55,120
[SPEAKER_02]: These one, the other.

287
00:25:55,160 --> 00:25:58,902
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you're genuinely mindful, the first thing you're going to recognize is, I don't know.

288
00:25:59,603 --> 00:26:04,806
[SPEAKER_02]: The second thing you can come to sort of recognize is, well, okay, what do I need to know then?

289
00:26:06,086 --> 00:26:08,628
[SPEAKER_02]: And then you sort of start to narrow it down.

290
00:26:09,568 --> 00:26:11,890
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't need to know everything.

291
00:26:11,910 --> 00:26:13,971
[SPEAKER_02]: You just need to know suffering and freedom from it.

292
00:26:15,031 --> 00:26:17,272
[SPEAKER_02]: So that's where your mindfulness should also be concerned with.

293
00:26:20,154 --> 00:26:21,735
[SPEAKER_02]: With your feeling, what feeling is present.

294
00:26:22,155 --> 00:26:23,656
[SPEAKER_02]: Then it will become apparent that actually,

295
00:26:25,781 --> 00:26:35,544
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, for example, as I mentioned, the idea of the the idea of the flux, the idea of how everything is constantly changing, doesn't make sense, because if everything's constantly changing, there wouldn't be any change at all.

296
00:26:36,225 --> 00:26:40,706
[SPEAKER_02]: So, when you're mindful, there is something, your experience is present as such.

297
00:26:41,367 --> 00:26:50,970
[SPEAKER_02]: It's mindful, it's enduring, which means, if you were to then say, oh, it's constantly changing, or any other wrong view of that kind, that would mean you're adopting an external explanation.

298
00:26:52,135 --> 00:26:54,438
[SPEAKER_02]: That it's not validated by your mindfulness.

299
00:26:54,458 --> 00:26:56,961
[SPEAKER_02]: Your mindfulness tells you the contrary.

300
00:26:56,981 --> 00:26:58,602
[SPEAKER_02]: Which means you then ignore your mindfulness.

301
00:26:59,403 --> 00:27:02,246
[SPEAKER_02]: To explain it based on the views that you accepted beforehand.

302
00:27:03,468 --> 00:27:11,356
[SPEAKER_02]: So then you want to stop doing that, really, and then develop mindfulness, and then the views that come from what's evident in your mindfulness.

303
00:27:11,376 --> 00:27:12,918
[SPEAKER_02]: There was the whole idea of phenomenology.

304
00:27:13,732 --> 00:27:22,896
[SPEAKER_02]: The, like, the philosophical movement of essential philosophy is basically describing only what's what's evident in a phenomenological outlook, which is the mindful outlook, mindfulness.

305
00:27:23,576 --> 00:27:28,339
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, if anything else would fall off, no matter how plausible it was, they would recognize it.

306
00:27:28,359 --> 00:27:30,960
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, that's an explanation.

307
00:27:31,000 --> 00:27:36,922
[SPEAKER_02]: And if it's an explanation, means it's not actually coming from the direct mindfulness, direct description, direct understanding.

308
00:27:36,983 --> 00:27:38,163
[SPEAKER_02]: As such, it can be accepted.

309
00:27:38,763 --> 00:27:42,405
[SPEAKER_02]: Obviously, phenomenologists and essential philosophers couldn't

310
00:27:43,372 --> 00:27:47,534
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, arrive at greater conclusions, but it's a valid method.

311
00:27:50,295 --> 00:28:00,499
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, in that case, in that, if you were to do it, you can't, like, you know, you can go off the tracks a bit, left to right, but you can't go off too far.

312
00:28:01,379 --> 00:28:05,261
[SPEAKER_02]: Because this principle of being based upon mindfulness is not abandoned.

313
00:28:05,561 --> 00:28:07,822
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it will always bring you back and restart.

314
00:28:09,998 --> 00:28:18,464
[SPEAKER_02]: But that means person will have to be willing to completely let go, challenge, abandon his own most personal views.

315
00:28:20,666 --> 00:28:24,428
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, let alone not get angry over it, or upset, or argumentative.

316
00:28:24,468 --> 00:28:37,037
[SPEAKER_02]: But really, genuinely, whenever he approaches to the new view, or to the view that sort of challenges his own view, he approaches it with a sense as something that might be right, and it might... has to be accepted.

317
00:28:37,738 --> 00:28:39,059
[SPEAKER_02]: Which means that what he has now,

318
00:28:39,747 --> 00:28:43,108
[SPEAKER_02]: Even if it's years of practice invested, are always in question.

319
00:28:44,048 --> 00:28:45,328
[SPEAKER_02]: Because that's what mindfulness is.

320
00:28:48,309 --> 00:28:52,070
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you do it like that, then the solid core will actually remain.

321
00:28:53,090 --> 00:29:00,392
[SPEAKER_02]: And you will be abandoning everything unnecessary, everything wrong, while the right things will stay there.

322
00:29:00,672 --> 00:29:00,872
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

323
00:29:00,952 --> 00:29:01,653
[SPEAKER_02]: Because they were right.

324
00:29:04,013 --> 00:29:04,713
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's not easy.

325
00:29:04,733 --> 00:29:06,634
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, you know, it comes to a point whereby

326
00:29:08,858 --> 00:29:20,963
[SPEAKER_02]: You experience fear by reading different views, by reading views that are challenging yours, by talking to people that are saying you're wrong.

327
00:29:21,523 --> 00:29:25,785
[SPEAKER_02]: Like genuine existential anxiety, fear.

328
00:29:25,825 --> 00:29:27,246
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not optional, holding views.

329
00:29:27,286 --> 00:29:28,066
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not optional.

330
00:29:29,280 --> 00:29:30,501
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, views are what you are.

331
00:29:30,801 --> 00:29:32,482
[SPEAKER_02]: It's kind of your own foundation.

332
00:29:32,522 --> 00:29:37,304
[SPEAKER_02]: So, challenging them feels like you're really shaking your own foundations.

333
00:29:37,384 --> 00:29:38,105
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you get angry.

334
00:29:38,305 --> 00:29:40,406
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, that's why most people get angry.

335
00:29:40,446 --> 00:29:40,866
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

336
00:29:40,886 --> 00:29:43,668
[SPEAKER_02]: If you say our meditation techniques are nonsense.

337
00:29:43,688 --> 00:29:44,768
[SPEAKER_02]: How dare you say that?

338
00:29:45,309 --> 00:29:47,190
[SPEAKER_02]: I've been meditating for 20 years.

339
00:29:47,610 --> 00:29:48,470
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, good for you.

340
00:29:49,591 --> 00:29:52,413
[SPEAKER_02]: Why do you get angry on account of it?

341
00:29:52,433 --> 00:29:55,234
[SPEAKER_02]: Meditation is supposed to free you from greed, aversion, delusion.

342
00:29:55,714 --> 00:29:56,815
[SPEAKER_02]: Why are you getting angry now?

343
00:29:58,676 --> 00:29:59,997
[SPEAKER_02]: Because of your meditation.

344
00:30:01,157 --> 00:30:02,418
[SPEAKER_02]: That's a contradiction in turn.

345
00:30:03,398 --> 00:30:09,501
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you either haven't been meditating rightly, since you're not free from anger, or the Buddha was wrong.

346
00:30:09,721 --> 00:30:10,602
[SPEAKER_02]: So, which one it is?

347
00:30:12,503 --> 00:30:13,843
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, we all know which one it is.

348
00:30:16,305 --> 00:30:27,070
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if I recognize that I don't have any mindfulness, and in order to develop it, if I keep reflecting as much as I can, like, as the moment passes,

349
00:30:28,280 --> 00:30:28,860
[SPEAKER_00]: See, there you go.

350
00:30:29,061 --> 00:30:29,861
[SPEAKER_00]: One moment passes.

351
00:30:29,981 --> 00:30:33,423
[SPEAKER_00]: Not one moment passes, but more like the moment that I passed.

352
00:30:34,423 --> 00:30:38,545
[SPEAKER_00]: Reflect, keep reflecting, like, on my actions, like, as fast as I can.

353
