538
00:44:10,784 --> 00:44:11,545
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

539
00:44:16,827 --> 00:44:20,868
[SPEAKER_02]: And you can see that none of the techniques you will be doing, in terms of meditation, would come under that.

540
00:44:21,858 --> 00:44:23,940
[SPEAKER_02]: What wisdom you're developing by watching your nostrils.

541
00:44:26,621 --> 00:44:28,102
[SPEAKER_01]: It makes you feel good.

542
00:44:28,643 --> 00:44:33,546
[SPEAKER_02]: So, really, it comes down to discernment, mental discernment, something that you cannot put into a method.

543
00:44:34,667 --> 00:44:40,091
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you need to constantly, oh, is what I'm doing, what I'm thinking, would that be harmed?

544
00:44:40,271 --> 00:44:48,517
[SPEAKER_02]: Does that imply, is that connected to harming others, harming me, harming both, harming neither?

545
00:44:49,547 --> 00:44:51,729
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, it doesn't seem it's harming neither.

546
00:44:52,309 --> 00:44:54,971
[SPEAKER_02]: But then, is it also making me discern things?

547
00:44:55,192 --> 00:44:56,633
[SPEAKER_02]: Is it making things clearer?

548
00:44:57,033 --> 00:45:02,898
[SPEAKER_02]: So, when I say feeling-feeling or when I say perception-perception, do I know where those things stand for clearer?

549
00:45:03,738 --> 00:45:06,761
[SPEAKER_02]: And the reason why I'm singling these things out is because those things are the five aggregates.

550
00:45:07,221 --> 00:45:12,185
[SPEAKER_02]: And the five aggregates are the ones affected with assumptions, with the wrong views, with upādāna.

551
00:45:15,082 --> 00:45:21,426
[SPEAKER_02]: So, even in your speech, in designation, that's all form of upādāna can be, if it stands for wrong things.

552
00:45:21,606 --> 00:45:23,847
[SPEAKER_02]: It expresses the wrong things, the wrong assumptions.

553
00:45:27,169 --> 00:45:37,655
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you want to have things clear for you, not explained, not laid out, like as a nice little tidy chart, a map of things, oh, this is what it is, this is what it is, you know.

554
00:45:38,015 --> 00:45:44,059
[SPEAKER_02]: You want to know exactly when you utter a designation, you know what it stands for, phenomenologically.

555
00:45:46,390 --> 00:45:47,030
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I reckon.

556
00:45:47,050 --> 00:45:50,273
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's what it means, like, developing discernment, developing wisdom.

557
00:45:50,333 --> 00:45:51,273
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why it's necessary.

558
00:45:51,914 --> 00:45:57,098
[SPEAKER_02]: And you have to keep thinking like that, because if you are, well, you would at least be striving in the right direction.

559
00:45:57,578 --> 00:46:09,186
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you don't see that as a criteria, developing that kind of wisdom, then, exactly, then you can, yeah, well, watching, watching my nostrils doesn't harm anyone, doesn't harm me, doesn't harm others, so it's great.

560
00:46:11,988 --> 00:46:13,509
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not developed wisdom.

561
00:46:13,589 --> 00:46:14,610
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, exactly, it doesn't.

562
00:46:17,986 --> 00:46:22,269
[SPEAKER_02]: And when it comes to developing wisdom and understanding, there is no method you can engage in, as I said before.

563
00:46:23,390 --> 00:46:24,531
[SPEAKER_02]: It just doesn't work like that.

564
00:46:27,253 --> 00:46:28,694
[SPEAKER_00]: To each individual.

565
00:46:31,277 --> 00:46:45,548
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, it's to... it's to each individual, and it's also... understanding and discernment of the nature of things precedes the designation, the nature of designation.

566
00:46:46,566 --> 00:46:52,328
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can hear the instruction, but then you're understanding what it pertains to.

567
00:46:52,348 --> 00:46:53,028
[SPEAKER_02]: You perceive it.

568
00:46:53,148 --> 00:46:55,229
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why the Buddha referred to it as miracle.

569
00:46:56,649 --> 00:47:02,071
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that connection is something, through your own efforts, can become apparent.

570
00:47:02,111 --> 00:47:03,551
[SPEAKER_02]: And you're solely responsible for that.

571
00:47:03,611 --> 00:47:09,173
[SPEAKER_02]: Which means, even this instruction, utterance of another, can go only so far.

572
00:47:11,659 --> 00:47:18,763
[SPEAKER_02]: But then, me designating you a set of methods and observances you go through, and then you just adhering to it.

573
00:47:20,164 --> 00:47:26,188
[SPEAKER_02]: But can that somehow magically lead you onto that connection that you yourself responsible connection of understanding?

574
00:47:26,248 --> 00:47:27,069
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't work.

575
00:47:27,589 --> 00:47:28,570
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is why it's a wrong view.

576
00:47:28,590 --> 00:47:29,250
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a fetter.

577
00:47:30,110 --> 00:47:33,272
[SPEAKER_02]: Sīlabhātaparamāsa.

578
00:47:33,332 --> 00:47:35,674
[SPEAKER_02]: Holding on to virtue and duty.

579
00:47:36,254 --> 00:47:37,515
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, people translate that.

580
00:47:37,535 --> 00:47:40,057
[SPEAKER_02]: Sīlabhātaparamāsa is holding on to rites and rituals.

581
00:47:40,836 --> 00:47:43,136
[SPEAKER_02]: Whereby, it means, oh, rites and rituals.

582
00:47:43,737 --> 00:47:45,377
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's kind of obvious.

583
00:47:45,537 --> 00:47:51,198
[SPEAKER_02]: We all know that, you know, bowing to the random stone shrine doesn't mean anything.

584
00:47:51,258 --> 00:47:53,219
[SPEAKER_02]: So, we all abandon that fetter.

585
00:47:53,259 --> 00:47:56,059
[SPEAKER_02]: We just do it for whatever reasons, but we know it's not the practice.

586
00:47:56,739 --> 00:47:59,400
[SPEAKER_02]: But actually, the pala is sīlabhāta-paramāsa.

587
00:48:00,340 --> 00:48:04,201
[SPEAKER_02]: Sīla is the virtue and duty.

588
00:48:04,221 --> 00:48:08,482
[SPEAKER_02]: Sīlabhāta-paramāsa means holding on to the sense of virtue and duty.

589
00:48:08,542 --> 00:48:09,462
[SPEAKER_02]: And basically,

590
00:48:11,183 --> 00:48:17,204
[SPEAKER_02]: Holding on to thinking that your sīla, your purity, your valid, genuine purity, is what practice is.

591
00:48:18,364 --> 00:48:27,306
[SPEAKER_02]: Holding on to your sense of duty as somebody who practices the Buddha's teaching, or I should be doing this, I should be meditating, I should be... Holding on to that to be the practice.

592
00:48:27,946 --> 00:48:28,906
[SPEAKER_02]: All of that is a fattah.

593
00:48:29,086 --> 00:48:29,286
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

594
00:48:29,326 --> 00:48:30,427
[SPEAKER_02]: Because that's not the practice.

595
00:48:31,027 --> 00:48:37,288
[SPEAKER_02]: And they did say, there is no dhamma without vinaya, but vinaya is not dhamma, and dhamma is not vinaya.

596
00:48:38,498 --> 00:48:42,440
[SPEAKER_02]: But obviously, you need vinaya in order to be able to practice dhamma correction.

597
00:48:43,861 --> 00:48:46,422
[SPEAKER_02]: But dhamma is not vinaya, dhamma is not sīla.

598
00:48:51,385 --> 00:48:56,147
[SPEAKER_02]: So, even your precepts that need to be pure, need to be kept, have nothing to do with dhamma.

599
00:48:56,847 --> 00:48:57,608
[SPEAKER_02]: You're gonna go like that.

600
00:48:57,728 --> 00:48:58,688
[SPEAKER_02]: Dhamma is elsewhere.

601
00:48:59,709 --> 00:49:00,810
[SPEAKER_02]: And if you look at it, why?

602
00:49:00,850 --> 00:49:01,550
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, it's obvious.

603
00:49:01,610 --> 00:49:03,951
[SPEAKER_02]: Because precepts are something you do, you observe.

604
00:49:04,832 --> 00:49:07,393
[SPEAKER_02]: Techniques are something you do, you perform.

605
00:49:08,555 --> 00:49:13,897
[SPEAKER_02]: But understanding of the nature of things is something you cannot do, you cannot perform.

606
00:49:16,118 --> 00:49:17,918
[SPEAKER_02]: This is a completely different domain.

607
00:49:18,699 --> 00:49:34,444
[SPEAKER_02]: Obviously, the understanding of the nature of things cannot happen if your behavior has not been purified externally through keeping the precepts and not engaging and maintaining unwholesome things through those bad habits.

608
00:49:38,508 --> 00:49:42,751
[SPEAKER_02]: But that purification through that behavior is not the understanding.

609
00:49:42,831 --> 00:49:47,854
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you can be as pure as, like, you know, anything that comes, but still not understand things.

610
00:49:48,354 --> 00:49:54,537
[SPEAKER_02]: So there is no guarantee of understanding through following methods, or observances, or duties.

611
00:49:55,638 --> 00:49:55,858
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

612
00:49:55,898 --> 00:49:57,419
[SPEAKER_02]: Because that's not what understanding is.

613
00:50:02,082 --> 00:50:07,165
[SPEAKER_02]: It's almost like, you know, that does not mean that, oh, I can do whatever I want and get the understanding.

614
00:50:09,575 --> 00:50:25,052
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, if you didn't understand things when things were favorable for you, when you were keeping the precepts, where you're not increasing the greed, aversion, delusion, well, then you're certainly not going to understand when your behavior is increasing greed, aversion, delusion.

615
00:50:25,953 --> 00:50:29,157
[SPEAKER_02]: Hence, it's a necessary prerequisite, but it's not it.

616
00:50:33,288 --> 00:50:45,436
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can see why then, like, thinking, oh, a certain prescribed technique, oh, can you, Ajahn, can you give me a technique or something I can do that will lead me to, you know, the magical place of Arahantship?

617
00:50:46,697 --> 00:50:51,520
[SPEAKER_02]: It's actually wrong, very, very seriously wrong view, because it becomes an impediment.

618
00:50:51,600 --> 00:50:56,443
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you think that you're actually practicing by observing this mechanical technique.

619
00:50:58,484 --> 00:51:00,205
[SPEAKER_02]: Which means that's where your efforts stop.

620
00:51:00,425 --> 00:51:02,607
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't stop and then start reflecting on that.

621
00:51:03,572 --> 00:51:06,854
[SPEAKER_02]: The nature of what you're doing, which is what understanding is.

622
00:51:06,914 --> 00:51:08,475
[SPEAKER_02]: No, you're just doing it, just doing it.

623
00:51:08,535 --> 00:51:14,519
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm just repeating, buddho, buddho, buddho, buddho, like a robot, and that brings me full understanding of stuff.

624
00:51:14,559 --> 00:51:15,120
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, right.

625
00:51:15,840 --> 00:51:23,105
[SPEAKER_02]: If that brings you the understanding, then the whole Tipiṭaka would be just buddho, buddho, buddho, buddho, buddho, buddho, buddho, wherever you open it, or whatever page you open it.

626
00:51:23,645 --> 00:51:25,006
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's not, not even once.

627
00:51:25,947 --> 00:51:28,769
[SPEAKER_02]: Never the Buddha say you just repeat this mantra, and

628
00:51:31,743 --> 00:51:37,545
[SPEAKER_02]: Many other followers and teachers of different sects and religions said that.

629
00:51:38,886 --> 00:51:40,286
[SPEAKER_02]: And they were all mentioned as wrong.

630
00:51:43,767 --> 00:51:43,907
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

631
00:51:43,927 --> 00:51:51,930
[SPEAKER_02]: Because nothing mechanical, such as repeating Buddha, Buddha, or watching the tip of your nose, can bring understanding.

632
00:51:52,390 --> 00:51:57,072
[SPEAKER_02]: The only thing that can bring understanding is your attitude that's trying to understand.

633
00:51:58,037 --> 00:52:02,118
[SPEAKER_02]: And that attitude can be in regard to anything that is established upon also.

634
00:52:02,698 --> 00:52:09,421
[SPEAKER_02]: Hence, the meditation techniques, well, they're wrong because people think them, take them to be right.

635
00:52:09,921 --> 00:52:11,821
[SPEAKER_02]: But in themselves, they're not even wrong.

636
00:52:12,221 --> 00:52:13,582
[SPEAKER_02]: They're absolutely pointless.

637
00:52:14,420 --> 00:52:16,141
[SPEAKER_02]: You can be watching your nostrils, or not.

638
00:52:16,441 --> 00:52:17,261
[SPEAKER_02]: What's the point?

639
00:52:17,281 --> 00:52:19,102
[SPEAKER_02]: You can watch the tips of your ears.

640
00:52:19,182 --> 00:52:28,245
[SPEAKER_01]: Like the Buddha, when he was teaching, he was talking about these things, certain things, you know, the five aggregates, the five elements, he's talking about those things.

641
00:52:28,605 --> 00:52:28,905
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

642
00:52:31,006 --> 00:52:33,567
[SPEAKER_01]: And people understand and get free from substance.

643
00:52:33,747 --> 00:52:37,148
[SPEAKER_02]: Because they're understanding the designations that he's using when he says.

644
00:52:37,409 --> 00:52:38,309
[SPEAKER_01]: He's not ringing a bell.

645
00:52:39,149 --> 00:52:41,090
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah, exactly.

646
00:52:42,367 --> 00:52:46,229
[SPEAKER_02]: And they will become arahants, right there and then, by listening to the Buddha speak.

647
00:52:47,090 --> 00:52:51,212
[SPEAKER_02]: Not by watching their nose for three hours, and then we go walking.

648
00:52:52,233 --> 00:52:54,534
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, understanding what he's talking about, isn't it?

649
00:52:54,574 --> 00:53:01,478
[SPEAKER_02]: Actually paying attention, trying to figure out... Paying attention to designations that he's using, and then seeing them for what they are, in their own experience.

650
00:53:05,261 --> 00:53:07,222
[SPEAKER_02]: They have the right attitude, trying to understand.

651
00:53:09,796 --> 00:53:16,678
[SPEAKER_02]: And the designations they themselves were operating with were not as polluted as most of other people today's designations are.

652
00:53:17,219 --> 00:53:21,340
[SPEAKER_02]: So then, it's nothing magical about people becoming arahants upon listening to Buddha speak.

653
00:53:22,100 --> 00:53:23,861
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, well, you know, the Buddha knew their mind.

654
00:53:23,881 --> 00:53:24,401
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, sure.

655
00:53:24,541 --> 00:53:26,122
[SPEAKER_02]: But that's not the reason why they became arahants.

656
00:53:26,142 --> 00:53:28,102
[SPEAKER_02]: They became arahants because they understood what he was saying.

657
00:53:30,779 --> 00:53:42,391
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if now you don't understand what the Buddha is saying in the suttas, it's because you just have so many obstructions and assumptions of your own doing, for which you're fully responsible, obscuring the meaning of the suttas.

658
00:53:43,672 --> 00:53:45,414
[SPEAKER_02]: So, undo your own assumptions.

659
00:53:45,574 --> 00:53:54,343
[SPEAKER_02]: Undo your own designations to the extent necessary, whereby you read the suttas and it's directly describing your experience.

660
00:53:55,377 --> 00:53:57,537
[SPEAKER_02]: When that's the case, 100% you'll be an arahant.

661
00:53:57,698 --> 00:54:01,198
[SPEAKER_02]: Because every sutta describes experience of an arahant, or how to become one.

662
00:54:07,080 --> 00:54:13,381
[SPEAKER_02]: If you read a sutta and it seems like common sense, like, oh yeah... Oh, that means you have some serious work to do.

663
00:54:14,801 --> 00:54:16,842
[SPEAKER_02]: And yeah, lots of people say, well, this is just common sense.

664
00:54:16,902 --> 00:54:17,842
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just repetition.

665
00:54:20,402 --> 00:54:22,683
[SPEAKER_02]: If you think of it just in terms that I said, imagine

666
00:54:23,472 --> 00:54:38,637
[SPEAKER_02]: If every designation that the Buddha would use stands for a direct description in your experience, then him repeating it through different aspects of it would be bringing it, in your experience, one aspect within the other, within the other, within...

667
00:54:38,697 --> 00:54:42,999
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can see it's almost like a natural progression of understanding.

668
00:54:43,019 --> 00:54:47,480
[SPEAKER_02]: Like fortifying it in regard to this, in regard to this, within that, within this.

669
00:54:47,500 --> 00:54:51,742
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you realize the suttas are far from just repetitive, eh...

670
00:54:52,670 --> 00:55:08,583
[SPEAKER_02]: kind of tautological folklore descriptions of something, it's actually direct phenomenological instruction and like leading on further and further and further in developing of your understanding and your purification of your own designations.

671
00:55:09,184 --> 00:55:11,046
[SPEAKER_02]: They were carrying wrong wrong assumptions.

672
00:55:13,127 --> 00:55:20,734
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you approach it directly, rather correctly, if you approach it correctly, with the right view,

673
00:55:21,992 --> 00:55:30,015
[SPEAKER_02]: And phenomenological mindfulness established, reading the suttas would be like directly purifying your own experience while you're reading them.

674
00:55:30,675 --> 00:55:31,535
[SPEAKER_02]: Because that's what they are.

675
00:55:33,076 --> 00:55:38,697
[SPEAKER_02]: Hence, repeating it for 20 times would be 20 repetitions of purifications.

676
00:55:38,737 --> 00:55:40,438
[SPEAKER_02]: 6 times, 12 times, 18 times.

677
00:55:40,938 --> 00:55:49,921
[SPEAKER_02]: No matter how many times it would repeat, you would retreat, and each time it would be a purified image of a correct designation, things standing correctly for what they are.

678
00:55:51,044 --> 00:56:04,432
[SPEAKER_02]: So, reading the suttas becomes the direct practice, the direct insight, which is how it was when the Buddha was speaking to those who became sotāpannas or arahants upon listening.

679
00:56:12,056 --> 00:56:15,718
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why repetition in the suttas, because he's not explaining.

680
00:56:17,625 --> 00:56:19,285
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a direct description of your experience.

681
00:56:19,325 --> 00:56:24,186
[SPEAKER_02]: And if he repeats it six times in regard to every sense, that's because... that's how it needs to be seen.

682
00:56:24,886 --> 00:56:26,867
[SPEAKER_02]: Repetitively, in regard to every sense.

683
00:56:27,387 --> 00:56:27,647
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

684
00:56:27,747 --> 00:56:30,727
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if you leave a sense unturned, ignorance will stay there.

685
00:56:45,130 --> 00:56:45,850
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, powerful.

686
00:56:47,990 --> 00:56:59,553
[SPEAKER_01]: I think we were talking about the other day about jhānas being, you know, by thinking and pondering in the correct way, one enters the jhānas.

687
00:57:00,253 --> 00:57:03,534
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, you know, you're secluded from unwholesome states.

688
00:57:04,514 --> 00:57:08,015
[SPEAKER_01]: You're guarding your mind, so you notice, I'm secluded from wholesome states.

689
00:57:08,976 --> 00:57:10,716
[SPEAKER_01]: Anything has to come through here, has to come through here.

690
00:57:11,096 --> 00:57:16,418
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm restraining my senses, my senses are guarded.

691
00:57:16,658 --> 00:57:17,658
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you enter, well,

692
00:57:18,180 --> 00:57:19,901
[SPEAKER_01]: They've become joy and so on.

693
00:57:20,121 --> 00:57:23,804
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's just... there is no blanking out, ever.

694
00:57:24,564 --> 00:57:25,225
[SPEAKER_02]: No, no, no, no.

695
00:57:25,785 --> 00:57:40,915
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, the sutta, one by one, I think it was 109, Madhyamaka, about Sariputta's, according to different, sort of, establishments of his mind, the first jhāna, second jhāna, third jhāna, and so on, all the way up to cessation of perception and feeling.

696
00:57:41,035 --> 00:57:43,977
[SPEAKER_02]: Every of those states had mindfulness present.

697
00:57:45,219 --> 00:58:01,307
[SPEAKER_02]: Just fortifying that same mindfulness, which is not, like, completely the opposite of what what people expect to achieve through various meditation techniques, when you're just not aware of anything.

698
00:58:02,328 --> 00:58:04,270
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just, oh, yeah, I went into this state...

699
00:58:04,370 --> 00:58:05,591
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm mindful, and then...

700
00:58:05,611 --> 00:58:13,697
[SPEAKER_02]: I went into this state of neither perception nor perception, because I don't... Gee, it was just this state I entered, and next thing I know, it was five o'clock in the morning.

701
00:58:13,717 --> 00:58:17,200
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like, yeah, we heard you're neither perception nor perception snoring.

702
00:58:17,840 --> 00:58:24,485
[SPEAKER_02]: Or like, oh, yeah, well, I go into this state when I don't hear or see anything.

703
00:58:24,646 --> 00:58:27,748
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a good meditation, no?

704
00:58:27,808 --> 00:58:29,009
[SPEAKER_01]: Not according to the circumstances.

705
00:58:30,034 --> 00:58:38,676
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, there are neither perception or perception of genuine kind, cessation of perception and feeling, but in order to get there, your mindfulness has to be utterly, completely unshakable.

706
00:58:42,597 --> 00:58:46,338
[SPEAKER_00]: But a lot of people refer to it as mindfulness as a phenomenon, isn't it?

707
00:58:47,798 --> 00:58:53,860
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, yeah, like, you know, I kind of, in the beginning, always giving the benefit of doubt, but then the more they talk about it, you realize you were just asleep, mate.

708
00:58:55,993 --> 00:59:03,939
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you know when you go in a state when you can't fall asleep because you're not lying down, but you're kind of half out.

709
00:59:04,900 --> 00:59:10,064
[SPEAKER_02]: Lots of people end up in that state because they're seated on the floor, so you can't quite fall asleep.

710
00:59:10,124 --> 00:59:10,945
[SPEAKER_01]: The twilight zone.

711
00:59:11,445 --> 00:59:17,930
[SPEAKER_02]: The twilight zone, which again, that jhāna is confirmed by kind of external eye of nodding.

712
00:59:18,190 --> 00:59:20,932
[SPEAKER_02]: We can see them nodding left and right, bobbing and weaving, sort of.

713
00:59:21,913 --> 00:59:23,294
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, yeah, I have a good meditation.

714
00:59:23,334 --> 00:59:24,535
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, we saw that.

715
00:59:30,335 --> 00:59:33,477
[SPEAKER_02]: So, again, it's the lack of a person being authentic.

716
00:59:35,919 --> 00:59:36,740
[SPEAKER_02]: They lie to themselves.

717
00:59:37,540 --> 00:59:44,545
[SPEAKER_02]: And, yeah, you know, quite a few people I've met were very sensitive about a topic of meditation.

718
00:59:44,605 --> 00:59:52,351
[SPEAKER_02]: If they would be implying that they're maybe not meditating rightly, or if somebody else would be implying indirectly that what they're doing is not right, they get very upset.

719
00:59:53,470 --> 00:59:57,771
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, well, what's the point of your meditation if you hold on to it so dearly?

720
00:59:57,791 --> 01:00:00,451
[SPEAKER_02]: And personally, you're getting upset if somebody criticizes you.

721
01:00:03,672 --> 01:00:04,952
[SPEAKER_02]: And surely, you're not doing it right.

722
01:00:07,473 --> 01:00:15,974
[SPEAKER_02]: If someone else's random view can shake you up so much, can offend you... You should be grateful in a way.

723
01:00:16,334 --> 01:00:17,434
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

724
01:00:17,454 --> 01:00:18,255
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, there you go.

725
01:00:18,295 --> 01:00:18,735
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't know.

726
01:00:19,075 --> 01:00:20,195
[SPEAKER_02]: I just laid it out for you.

727
01:00:29,200 --> 01:00:39,327
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, like, if I read this, Roman philosophers, Greek philosophers, they sought out challenges, you know.

728
01:00:39,367 --> 01:00:45,792
[SPEAKER_02]: You sought out a challenge of your view, not the challenge to prove your sīla is good or something like that, which sometimes people do.

729
01:00:46,312 --> 01:00:47,873
[SPEAKER_02]: You want to challenge your views.

730
01:00:47,953 --> 01:00:48,474
[SPEAKER_02]: Absolutely.

731
01:00:49,202 --> 01:01:02,446
[SPEAKER_02]: So, someone says, oh, tell me your view... That's the attitude you can be sure about, that can't lead you to wrong, is attitude of challenging every view, no matter how dear to you it is.

732
01:01:03,347 --> 01:01:03,767
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's it.

733
01:01:03,787 --> 01:01:05,007
[SPEAKER_02]: The views are dear to you.

734
01:01:05,187 --> 01:01:08,729
[SPEAKER_02]: The Buddha said, ah, surely you're holding to that view because it pleases you.

735
01:01:08,789 --> 01:01:09,509
[SPEAKER_02]: It's dear to you.

736
01:01:11,209 --> 01:01:17,492
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, when that man said, I said to the Buddha, I don't hold to any views.

737
01:01:17,552 --> 01:01:18,792
[SPEAKER_02]: No views are pleasing to me.

738
01:01:19,833 --> 01:01:21,974
[SPEAKER_02]: And he said, well, surely that view is pleasing to you.

739
01:01:24,395 --> 01:01:26,716
[SPEAKER_02]: And you say, oh, okay, yeah, you have a point there.

740
01:01:30,658 --> 01:01:40,162
[SPEAKER_02]: But when you have a view, that no view will be dear to you, including that view, in a sense, that whatever view you have, you recognize you always have to have a view.

741
01:01:40,542 --> 01:01:43,703
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't have a state of no view, because that's a view.

742
01:01:44,664 --> 01:01:47,465
[SPEAKER_02]: Which means, if you believe it's not the view, you're contradicting yourself.

743
01:01:48,125 --> 01:01:49,226
[SPEAKER_02]: Thus, you're even more wrong.

744
01:01:50,830 --> 01:01:53,811
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's not about not having any views, which is impossible.

745
01:01:53,831 --> 01:02:00,432
[SPEAKER_02]: It's about finding the right view, and then going beyond even the right view, which is possible, which is arahantship.

746
01:02:01,672 --> 01:02:06,873
[SPEAKER_02]: But in order to get the right view, you must not hold to any view for the sake of pleasing you.

747
01:02:07,353 --> 01:02:08,573
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, this view is pleasing to me.

748
01:02:09,494 --> 01:02:12,174
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't help having a view being pleasing to you.

749
01:02:13,374 --> 01:02:16,815
[SPEAKER_02]: But what you can help is not leaving it unturned.

750
01:02:18,075 --> 01:02:21,358
[SPEAKER_02]: Not take it for granted, not leaving it unquestioned.

751
01:02:22,239 --> 01:02:26,363
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you get a view, then you find the views that are opposing to that view.

752
01:02:27,844 --> 01:02:34,070
[SPEAKER_02]: And if you think, oh, this is a wrong view, well, you need to fully understand why, how and where that view is wrong.

753
01:02:35,271 --> 01:02:38,253
[SPEAKER_02]: Not just decide this is wrong because somebody else said, even the Buddha.

754
01:02:39,194 --> 01:02:41,997
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, the Buddha said that, well, sure, but do you understand it for yourself?

755
01:02:44,313 --> 01:02:46,054
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you understand why wrong views are wrong?

756
01:02:46,254 --> 01:02:47,855
[SPEAKER_02]: If you do, you have the right views.

757
01:02:49,296 --> 01:02:50,777
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's dang... so it's...

758
01:02:50,877 --> 01:02:57,040
[SPEAKER_01]: I think it's probably... well, it is useful if someone... you know, someone who can challenge you, you know.

759
01:02:57,421 --> 01:02:57,641
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

760
01:02:57,961 --> 01:02:58,241
[SPEAKER_01]: You know.

761
01:02:58,401 --> 01:03:01,503
[SPEAKER_02]: If you don't have anybody challenging you, then open any... any book.

762
01:03:01,643 --> 01:03:02,584
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, challenge yourself.

763
01:03:02,704 --> 01:03:06,386
[SPEAKER_02]: Open... because you're bound to think, oh, these... these views are wrong.

764
01:03:06,426 --> 01:03:11,929
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, open those books, read them, and try to see for yourself why, how and where those views are wrong.

765
01:03:12,309 --> 01:03:13,470
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, just assume they're wrong.

766
01:03:14,455 --> 01:03:14,935
[SPEAKER_02]: And same.

767
01:03:15,456 --> 01:03:16,196
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, the Buddha is right.

768
01:03:16,256 --> 01:03:16,516
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay.

769
01:03:17,037 --> 01:03:19,718
[SPEAKER_02]: Open the suttas and understand where, how and why it's right.

770
01:03:22,100 --> 01:03:23,761
[SPEAKER_02]: And if you're doing it correctly, you'll see.

771
01:03:23,981 --> 01:03:24,861
[SPEAKER_02]: You'll be fearful.

772
01:03:25,041 --> 01:03:33,166
[SPEAKER_02]: You'll be, like, anxious to read other stuff that you think is wrong, because you might not see it is wrong.

773
01:03:33,406 --> 01:03:35,848
[SPEAKER_02]: Thus, you're not as right as you think you are.

774
01:03:36,368 --> 01:03:37,288
[SPEAKER_02]: But that's inevitable.

775
01:03:37,308 --> 01:03:40,370
[SPEAKER_02]: You have to go through that.

776
01:03:41,091 --> 01:03:43,032
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so let's take the thing that you

777
01:03:44,262 --> 01:03:51,145
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if there is anything right, it's that which cannot be taken personally, and it will stay.

778
01:03:53,626 --> 01:03:54,267
[SPEAKER_02]: It will stay on.

779
01:03:54,927 --> 01:03:55,627
[SPEAKER_02]: It won't drop off.

780
01:03:57,888 --> 01:04:00,829
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it can't be... no one can challenge it, really.

781
01:04:01,170 --> 01:04:02,650
[SPEAKER_01]: No one can take it away.

782
01:04:02,710 --> 01:04:04,371
[SPEAKER_02]: You've been challenging it.

783
01:04:04,391 --> 01:04:08,653
[SPEAKER_02]: You've been, like, attacking it with doubt.

784
01:04:09,917 --> 01:04:13,559
[SPEAKER_02]: And whatever was susceptible to doubt was affectable.

785
01:04:14,420 --> 01:04:16,040
[SPEAKER_02]: Except those things that were beyond doubt.

786
01:04:17,801 --> 01:04:24,065
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you go beyond doubt by basically reaching a point where the doubt cannot enter.

787
01:04:24,265 --> 01:04:28,788
[SPEAKER_02]: Not because you were shying away from doubting it, but because you were doubting full on.

788
01:04:29,588 --> 01:04:30,729
[SPEAKER_02]: You fully became doubt.

789
01:04:31,789 --> 01:04:35,311
[SPEAKER_02]: And then you recognize that there are points the doubt cannot enter.

790
01:04:35,731 --> 01:04:36,732
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's doubt free.

791
01:04:37,312 --> 01:04:38,193
[SPEAKER_02]: It's beyond doubt.

792
01:04:39,563 --> 01:04:41,785
[SPEAKER_02]: Beyond doubt means inaccessible to doubt.

793
01:04:42,306 --> 01:04:46,109
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't mean, oh, I never doubt this because, you know, I fear.

794
01:04:47,290 --> 01:04:55,778
[SPEAKER_02]: I fear that if I doubt, I might change my mind, re-evaluate everything and, phew, 30 years that I invest in this, down the drain.

795
01:04:56,018 --> 01:04:56,178
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

796
01:04:56,799 --> 01:04:58,781
[SPEAKER_02]: From the day one, full on doubt.

797
01:04:59,621 --> 01:05:05,607
[SPEAKER_02]: Very unpleasant, but, you know, relatively quick way of progressing if you do it right.

798
01:05:10,441 --> 01:05:12,565
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you know, oh, this is beyond doubt.

799
01:05:13,187 --> 01:05:18,838
[SPEAKER_02]: Not because I'm not doubting it, but because its nature is impenetrable to doubt.

